WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.080
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Mike Rinder, who is a former senior executive

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.210
of the Church of Scientology, as well as the
C organization.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.420
Mike, it's so great to have you on today.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.190
Speaker 6: Thanks, David, it's lovely to be
here.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:19.980
Speaker 1: So I've interviewed both first
and second generation Scientology members,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.250
I know that often the dynamics for one's relationship
to Scientology are different, depending on

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:31.189
whether your parents were already part of
it when you were born or not.

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:32.910
Talk to us a little bit about your path in.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:40.910
Speaker 6: OK, I'm second generation Scientologist,
my parents got involved in Scientology in

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:47.100
Australia when Hubbard went there to do some
lectures in 1959.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:54.550
And so from a very young age, I was raised
in a Scientology family.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:01:00.760
And then once I finished high school, I joined
what's called the Sea Organization, which

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:12.600
is the like the elite in a core of Scientology,
people that devote their entire lives to supposedly

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.530
achieving the aims of the religion.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:23.230
They sign a billion year contract because
Scientologists believe that you live many

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:27.700
different lifetimes and your spirit transcends
bodies.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:33.680
And so you commit yourself basically to an
eternity of service of Scientology.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:40.250
And I did that from the age of 18 until I
left when I was 53.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:46.030
Speaker 1: So there's already five branching
paths we could go down here.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:51.700
Let's let's address a few maybe of the sort
of nuts and bolts you say when you finished

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:59.470
high school, a Scientology big on people that
are part of the Sea Org not going to college.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.330
Speaker 6: Absolutely.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:02.330
Hubbard.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:07.610
I mean, everything in Scientology is based
on what Hubbard's view of things are.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:17.950
And Hubbard had a very negative opinion of
educational systems and educational institutions,

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:21.100
partly because he dropped out of college.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:30.810
But also he figured that he had come up with
a, quote, technology of study that made everything

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.450
else sort of irrelevant.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:39.599
And because he believed that Scientology addresses
everything there is to know about life and

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:47.340
the world, that it was far more important
for children to be educated in Scientology

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:53.610
than educated in, quote, warg meaning non
Scientology stuff.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.840
Speaker 1: Got it.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.760
And then in terms of the Sea Org, I know that
other Sea Org former Sea Org folks I've spoken

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:07.190
to say they got no salary, they basically
got housing.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:12.500
And then that was part of the way that the
church would exert control over them.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:13.830
Was that your situation?

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:19.890
Speaker 6: Yeah, absolutely, David, that's
everybody who was in the sea organization

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:27.720
is becomes basically an indentured servant
to the to the Organization of Scientology.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:37.520
In other words, you live you eat your health
care, whatever it may be, is provided by Scientology.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.430
You don't earn money per say.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:48.930
You get what's called a stipend, which can
range from nothing to fifty dollars a week.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:55.620
Maybe if you're lucky and you so you have
no resources available.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.810
You most see your members do not own a vehicle.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.530
They don't own a house.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.400
They don't have any sort of worldly means.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.379
Many of them joined the sea organization even
younger than I did.

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:12.940
They don't even have a high school diploma.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:22.120
They don't have any anything that allows them
or would allow them to walk out into the real

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.139
world and carry on a normal life.

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:34.020
So the fear within the sea organization and
and it's something that is cultivated is that

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:41.110
stepping outside of the organization is going
to be tantamount to committing suicide.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:46.930
You know, there's this old joke, not so much
a joke, but a statement that goes around in

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:53.050
the in the Sea Org is if you leave the Sea
Org, all you'll do is end up living under

00:04:53.050 --> 00:05:00.979
a bridge or flipping burgers at McDonald's
that you don't have any any other avenue to

00:05:00.979 --> 00:05:04.569
pursue in life other than what we are offering
you.

00:05:04.569 --> 00:05:12.259
Speaker 1: So at some point you start to work
your way up and you start to actually you

00:05:12.259 --> 00:05:19.389
eventually become chief spokesperson and representative
of Scientology to the media at that point,

00:05:19.389 --> 00:05:21.599
or maybe put it a different way.

00:05:21.599 --> 00:05:27.379
At what point did you start to have doubts
about your everything that you were doing

00:05:27.379 --> 00:05:28.599
and what you had been told?

00:05:28.599 --> 00:05:37.870
Speaker 6: I started to have doubts probably
about five years before I left and I left

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.869
in 2007 in the early 2000s.

00:05:41.869 --> 00:05:48.629
But you have to understand, David, that having
doubts is there is this enormous push and

00:05:48.629 --> 00:05:54.009
pull of what you've been taught in Scientology.

00:05:54.009 --> 00:06:00.669
And there is a very fundamental principle
that Hubbard uses to hang on to people, which

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:09.559
is that your condition, whatever whatever
condition you find yourself in is your own.

00:06:09.559 --> 00:06:14.990
You've caused that that you've caused if it's
a bad state of affairs, you've caused it by

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:17.319
doing bad things.

00:06:17.319 --> 00:06:25.349
And so when you and it even is down to the
level of if you're contemplating leaving this

00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:29.550
organization, it means that you've done bad
things to this organization.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.229
So you get this push and pull.

00:06:32.229 --> 00:06:34.410
Oh, my God, something's wrong here.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:41.499
Oh, my Scientology teaching tells me that
that's because I've done something bad and

00:06:41.499 --> 00:06:46.490
I need to look inside within myself and figure
out what that is.

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:50.789
So and there are a lot of other factors that
go into it.

00:06:50.789 --> 00:06:53.520
Like I had two children and a wife.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:07:03.119
If I decide I wish to leave, then that becomes
a problem, because if I tell my wife, she's

00:07:03.119 --> 00:07:06.879
probably going to report me and then I'll
be stopped.

00:07:06.879 --> 00:07:13.360
If I don't tell her, I'm going to lose my
wife and children.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.189
So there's a and and that's just the sort
of most surface factors.

00:07:19.189 --> 00:07:23.689
Speaker 1: So talk a little bit about the
concept I've heard before about I will be

00:07:23.689 --> 00:07:25.240
prevented from leaving.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:30.310
And what I want to understand is as a general
thing, someone calls the police and says they're

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:35.659
being held against their will, for example,
and police show up and, you know, you don't

00:07:35.659 --> 00:07:38.150
even really need a warrant in that situation.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:41.629
You're able to verify someone's well-being.

00:07:41.629 --> 00:07:43.460
Did you even have access to a phone?

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.770
Could you even call police if you are being
held against your will?

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:50.219
Speaker 6: Well, this this depends on where
you're at.

00:07:50.219 --> 00:07:56.449
If I saw where I was at Golden Era Productions,
the international headquarters of Scientology

00:07:56.449 --> 00:08:02.929
in Riverside County, you don't have commonly
access to a phone and the phones that are

00:08:02.929 --> 00:08:07.919
there in order to be able to make any call,
you have to go via reception, I say.

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:17.360
But beneath that, David, is there is another
very well inculcated idea in Scientology that

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:24.400
going to the police or law enforcement is
a big no no, this is just not done.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:35.520
Nobody goes to call the police or do something
that is outside of the world of Scientology

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.520
to solve a problem within Scientology.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:44.810
Now, obviously, if you're if you're at the
point where you're ready to jump the fence

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:49.940
and run down the the highway in the middle
of nowhere and hope that someone is going

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:54.400
to pick you up, then you're not in that mindset.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.950
But it takes a lot of.

00:08:57.950 --> 00:09:07.900
A lot of thinking outside the bubble that
you exist in to get to that point right and

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:15.510
bite by very definition, because you're in
a bubble of agreed upon thoughts and ideas

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:21.140
and everybody else agrees with you and what's
right and what's wrong is all agreed upon.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:24.260
It's very difficult to think outside that
bubble.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:32.370
So that's that's why Larry writes so cleverly
called his book Going Clear Scientology and

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:38.900
the Prison of Belief, because though there
are security guards, there's lights, there's

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:44.620
motion sensors on the fences, there's cameras
everywhere, et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:49.840
The real prison of Scientology and particularly
service members is here.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.380
Speaker 1: Right.

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:58.410
And did you hear some of the former Scientology
people I've spoken to started to take issue

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:04.830
with the practices as an organization, but
almost continued having the doctrine beliefs

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.960
even after they left, were your doubts with
one the other or both?

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:14.620
Speaker 6: With primarily one the the organization
and David Miscavige,

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:20.530
Speaker 1: interesting, so your initial problem
was not you didn't believe the that aliens

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:25.340
previously were here or you weren't doubting
that you were doubting the organization.

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:26.840
Speaker 6: Correct.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:27.840
Why?

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:33.110
It takes and you know, I obviously have spoken
to a lot of former Scientologists, a lot of

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:35.340
former Sea Org members.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:45.870
It's sort of like a stage of peeling the onion,
of unburdening yourself, of ideas that have

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:54.130
been sort of built into your DNA, almost particularly
if you're raised that way.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:11:03.330
And the easiest ones are the ones that have
a very physical manifestation, like being

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:10.750
beaten up by David Miscavige is something
that's a very, you know, tangible here now

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:12.090
thing.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:20.110
The ideas of that Dianetics and Hubbard's
book, Dianetics is a load of bunk, takes a

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:29.340
lot longer to get sort of shed because it's
it's not so it's not so tangible.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:37.680
Speaker 1: That makes a lot of sense and I
mean, when when you it seems as though it

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:46.980
takes in a sense more, although in a physical
sense, the decision to leave or try to leave

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:50.130
seems like the piece that's most difficult.

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:53.390
It seems that may be the beliefs that have
been ingrained in you your entire life are

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:55.830
really the most difficult, although in a different
way.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:59.890
Speaker 6: Yeah, you're you're absolutely
right, David.

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:04.940
And I don't think that there is anybody that
you will ever talk to who is a former Scientologist

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:09.100
that will tell you anything different than
that other experience.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:15.380
Speaker 1: You talk talk about what it means
to be in the hole and your experience in the

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:17.150
hole for two years.

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:22.100
Speaker 6: It's well, it's really a prison.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:31.260
It was a prison that was created at the international
headquarters by David Miscavige in a double

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:35.790
wide trailer, a building that was constructed
out of two double wide trailers.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:40.540
And the doors were bugged and the windows
were screwed shut.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:45.820
And there was a security guard at the only.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:55.450
Access point to the building and we were put
in there to figure out why we were suppressive

00:12:55.450 --> 00:13:02.480
persons, suppressive person in Scientology
means someone who is intent upon destroying

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:03.480
mankind.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:04.480
Right.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:11.980
And Miscavige, David Miscavige, the the ecclesiastical
leader of the Scientology religion, as he

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:18.110
likes to call himself, was.

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:28.910
Always seeking to be the person who was the
dog, the big dog, the dominant personality

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:38.510
in the environment, and kept anybody who was
a potential threat to his control of the organization

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:42.110
constantly in a state of am I in trouble?

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:43.140
Am I not in trouble?

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:48.470
Am I going to be sent to to shovel in Louisiana?

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:51.990
Am I am I in good graces today?

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:58.600
And the whole was sort of the final manifestation
of this, where he basically took everybody

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:05.060
who was at the senior echelon of Scientology
and put them in this prison and said, when

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:11.050
you guys figure out why you're so bad, maybe
you can get out.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:12.050
Right.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:18.060
And that devolved in this, you know.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:24.040
Going clear gives a pretty good depiction
of some of what happened, the Alex Gibney

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:31.010
movie, the HBO movie, but the the the truth
of the matter is it devolved into a sort of

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:40.990
a Lord of the Flies world of people striving
to demonstrate to David Miscavige that they

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:49.510
were more on his team than anybody else by
attacking the other people and saying, you

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:55.730
know, you're a bad person or beating them
up or yelling at them or, you know, famously

00:14:55.730 --> 00:15:00.380
in the case of Debbie Cook, who was put there
and testified on the stand that she was put

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:06.160
in a garbage can with a sign around her neck
and people poured water over until she was

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:13.260
shivering in and being physically and verbally
abused.

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:17.960
This happened as the sort of.

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:26.029
Routine that ended up being in the hole and
it was pretty pretty.

00:15:26.029 --> 00:15:37.620
Both bizarre and horrifying and shocking and
a little unbelievable to think that that could

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:42.150
be happening in today's United States of America.

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:46.070
Speaker 1: Yeah, well, I think that's that's
the question a lot of people in the audience

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:48.070
have, which is how is how is it happening?

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:51.190
We're going to continue our conversation and
the full conversation will be posted on our

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:52.190
YouTube channel.

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:55.550
We're going to go to a break on the podcast,
TV and radio show.

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:59.970
We've been speaking, of course, with Mike
Rinder, a former senior executive of the Church

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.160
of Scientology and the Sea Org.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:04.589
The full conversation will be on our YouTube
channel

